// The transitive half of the sendability gate. `channel_send_shared` covers a bare `Shared<Leaf>` FIELD;
// this covers the same refcount one level deeper, inside a collection — which passed the gate until now.
//
// Why it escaped, then: a library generic keeps its element in its TYPE ARGUMENTS, not in a field a walk
// can resolve. `DynamicArray<T>` stores `UnsafePtr<T> data`, and a raw pointer is not a class, so the
// structural walk found nothing. Now the container states its own rule — `Sendable when [T: Sendable]` in
// std::collections — so `DynamicArray<Shared<Leaf>>` simply is not Sendable, and `Bad`'s declaration over
// it is refused naming the field `xs` and the argument that failed the gate.
//
// The hazard is identical to the guarded case: two isolates holding handles to one `Leaf` race a
// non-atomic `Rc` counter, so the pointee is freed twice or never. `channel_send_shared`'s own header
// already promised "transitively reaches a NON-ATOMIC shared refcount" — this makes that word true.
import { std::concurrent::Channel, std::concurrent::Sender, std::concurrent::Receiver, std::memory::Shared, std::collections::DynamicArray };

type value Leaf { int32 v; }

type resource Bad implements Sendable {
    DynamicArray<Shared<Leaf>> xs;   // the refcount, one indirection down
}

fn int32 main() {
    Channel<Bad> ch = Channel.bounded(capacity: 2);   // ERROR: `Bad`'s field `xs` reaches a shared refcount
    return 0;
}
